CAT = "cenCardio"; SEG = "CAR"
Q = [
("A patient with chest pain arrives in the emergency department. Within how many minutes of arrival should a 12-lead ECG be done?",
 "10 minutes", ["30 minutes", "60 minutes", "5 minutes after labs"],
 "A 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of arrival lets the team spot an ST-elevation MI quickly and start reperfusion. Waiting for lab results delays that decision."),
("A 12-lead ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF. Which wall of the heart is most likely injured?",
 "Inferior wall", ["Anterior wall", "Lateral wall", "Septal wall"],
 "Leads II, III and aVF look at the inferior surface, which is usually supplied by the right coronary artery. Anterior changes show in V3 and V4."),
("A patient with an inferior MI may also have right ventricular infarction. Which ECG lead best shows it?",
 "Lead V4R", ["Lead aVL", "Lead V1 only", "Lead I"],
 "A right-sided ECG places V4 on the right chest; ST elevation in V4R points to right ventricular infarction. Those patients are preload dependent, so nitrates are used with care."),
("A patient describes sudden tearing chest pain that radiates to the back, with a 30 mmHg difference in arm blood pressures. What does this most suggest?",
 "Aortic dissection", ["Stable angina", "Acute pericarditis", "Esophageal spasm"],
 "Tearing pain into the back and a pressure gap between the arms point to dissection of the aorta. Anticoagulants and thrombolytics are dangerous until it is ruled out."),
("For a suspected acute aortic dissection, what is the first goal of medication therapy?",
 "Lower heart rate and pressure", ["Dissolve the clot quickly", "Raise preload with fluids", "Increase cardiac contractility"],
 "Beta blockade is started first to slow the heart and reduce the force on the aortic wall, then pressure is lowered further. This limits extension of the tear."),
("During CPR, how long may compressions pause for a rhythm or pulse check?",
 "No more than 10 seconds", ["Up to 30 seconds", "About 1 minute", "As long as needed"],
 "Pauses cut coronary and brain blood flow, so rhythm and pulse checks are kept under 10 seconds."),
("During CPR the monitor shows ventricular fibrillation. What is the priority action?",
 "Defibrillate right away", ["Give epinephrine first", "Start transcutaneous pacing", "Give synchronized cardioversion"],
 "Ventricular fibrillation is a shockable rhythm, and survival falls with every minute without a shock. Pacing has no role in VF, and synchronized shocks need a QRS to sync to."),
("A patient in cardiac arrest is in asystole. Which intervention is appropriate?",
 "CPR and epinephrine", ["Immediate defibrillation", "Synchronized cardioversion", "Adenosine IV push"],
 "Asystole is not shockable. Treatment is high-quality CPR, epinephrine and a search for reversible causes."),
("A stable patient has a narrow, regular tachycardia at 190 beats per minute. Vagal maneuvers fail. Which drug is usually given next?",
 "Adenosine", ["Amiodarone", "Atropine", "Digoxin"],
 "Adenosine briefly blocks the AV node and often ends reentrant SVT. It is pushed rapidly through a proximal line and followed by a saline flush."),
("How should adenosine be given for supraventricular tachycardia?",
 "Rapid IV push with a flush", ["Slow push over 5 minutes", "Infusion over 1 hour", "Deep intramuscular injection"],
 "Adenosine has a half-life of seconds, so a slow push is cleared before it reaches the heart. A rapid push followed at once by a saline flush is used."),
("A patient has a heart rate of 38, a blood pressure of 76/40 and confusion. What is the first-line drug?",
 "Atropine", ["Adenosine", "Diltiazem", "Metoprolol"],
 "Atropine blocks vagal tone and is first-line for symptomatic bradycardia. Pacing or a dopamine or epinephrine infusion follows if it fails."),
("A patient with new atrial fibrillation and a rapid rate is unstable, with hypotension and chest pain. What is the indicated treatment?",
 "Synchronized cardioversion", ["Unsynchronized defibrillation", "Oral rate control", "Observation and rest"],
 "An unstable patient with a tachycardia that has a pulse needs a synchronized shock, timed to the QRS so it does not land on the T wave."),
("A patient has chest pain that improves when sitting up and leaning forward, with diffuse ST elevation. What does this suggest?",
 "Pericarditis", ["Inferior MI", "Pulmonary embolism", "Aortic stenosis"],
 "Pericarditis pain is often sharp, positional and eased by leaning forward. Its ST elevation is widespread rather than limited to one coronary territory."),
("Which finding is part of Beck's triad in pericardial tamponade?",
 "Muffled heart sounds", ["Widened pulse pressure", "Bounding peripheral pulses", "A loud S3 gallop"],
 "Beck's triad is hypotension, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Tamponade narrows the pulse pressure rather than widening it."),
("A patient with a stab wound to the chest has hypotension, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Which procedure relieves the cause?",
 "Pericardiocentesis", ["Needle thoracostomy", "Chest tube placement", "Cricothyrotomy"],
 "These are signs of tamponade, blood in the pericardial sac squeezing the heart. Draining the sac restores filling; definitive repair follows in surgery."),
("A patient with heart failure has crackles, frothy sputum and severe dyspnea. Which position helps most?",
 "Upright, legs dangling", ["Flat with legs raised", "Trendelenburg, head down", "Left side-lying flat"],
 "Sitting upright with the legs dependent pools blood in the legs, reducing venous return and pulmonary congestion. Raising the legs adds to the fluid load."),
("Which noninvasive treatment quickly improves breathing in acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema?",
 "CPAP or BiPAP", ["High-flow nasal oxygen only", "Incentive spirometry", "Chest physiotherapy"],
 "Positive pressure ventilation pushes fluid out of the alveoli, reduces preload and afterload and lowers the work of breathing. It often avoids intubation."),
("A patient's blood pressure is 220/130 with new confusion and papilledema. How much should the mean arterial pressure fall in the first hour?",
 "By about 25% at most", ["Back to normal at once", "By at least 50%", "Not at all for 24 hours"],
 "In a hypertensive emergency, a drop of no more than about 25% in the first hour protects organs that are used to high pressure. Lowering it to normal fast can cause stroke or ischemia."),
("A patient has a cold, pale, pulseless leg with sudden pain and numbness. What is the most likely cause?",
 "Acute arterial occlusion", ["Deep vein thrombosis", "Chronic venous insufficiency", "Superficial phlebitis"],
 "Pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia and poikilothermia point to a blocked artery. A DVT leaves the leg warm, swollen and with pulses."),
("A stable patient with normal kidney function is suspected of having a pulmonary embolism. Which test usually confirms it?",
 "CT pulmonary angiography", ["Chest x-ray alone", "Echocardiogram alone", "Upper GI series"],
 "CT pulmonary angiography shows clots in the pulmonary arteries directly. A chest x-ray is often normal in PE, and a V/Q scan is used when contrast must be avoided."),
("A patient with chest trauma shows ST changes and new arrhythmias after striking a steering wheel. What injury should the nurse suspect?",
 "Blunt cardiac injury", ["Tension pneumothorax", "Simple rib contusion", "Diaphragm rupture"],
 "A blow to the anterior chest can bruise the myocardium and cause dysrhythmias. Continuous cardiac monitoring is needed."),
("A patient with an MI develops hypotension, cool clammy skin and crackles. What type of shock is present?",
 "Cardiogenic shock", ["Hypovolemic shock", "Neurogenic shock", "Anaphylactic shock"],
 "Cardiogenic shock is pump failure: low output with high filling pressure, so the lungs fill with fluid. Hypovolemic shock has clear lungs and flat neck veins."),
("Which type of shock is caused by a mechanical block to blood flow, such as a tension pneumothorax?",
 "Obstructive shock", ["Distributive shock", "Hypovolemic shock", "Cardiogenic shock"],
 "Obstructive shock comes from something blocking flow into or out of the heart: tension pneumothorax, tamponade or a massive pulmonary embolus. Relieving the block is the treatment."),
("A patient with a history of IV drug use has fever, a new murmur and painless red spots on the palms. What does this suggest?",
 "Infective endocarditis", ["Rheumatic arthritis", "Kawasaki disease", "Acute viral pericarditis"],
 "Fever with a new murmur and Janeway lesions (painless palm and sole spots) suggests endocarditis. Blood cultures are drawn before antibiotics."),
]
